UPDATE public.designers
SET biography = $$Sebastian Herkner is among the most celebrated designers of his generation, a German-born voice whose work has come to define a warmer, more materially expressive current in contemporary design. Born in 1981 in Bad Mergentheim and trained at the Hochschule für Gestaltung in Offenbach am Main, he studied under Peter Ghyczy before an internship with Stella McCartney in London sharpened his instinct for tactility, colour and craft. He founded Studio Sebastian Herkner in Offenbach in 2006, and has since built one of Europe's most quietly influential practices — dividing his time between his own studio and long collaborations with editors across Germany, Italy, Denmark, France and beyond.

Herkner's language is deceptively simple. Precise, generous silhouettes are paired with an almost obsessive attention to materials — mouth-blown glass, patinated brass, hand-loomed textiles, solid woods, ceramics and stone — often sourced from small workshops he has visited personally. His breakthrough Bell Table for ClassiCon (2012), a blown-glass volume crowned by a slender metal disc, became an instant modern classic and set the tone for a career built on dialogue with makers rather than on formal provocation. Subsequent pieces for Moroso, Thonet, Dedon, Ames, Pulpo, &Tradition, Fontana Arte, Linteloo, Wittmann and many others have shared the same conviction: that industrial design can carry the intimacy of the hand.

For <a href="/designers/man-of-parts">Man of Parts</a>, Herkner has designed some of the studio's most poetic pieces — including the Praia da Granja Coffee Table and the Frenchmen Street Chair — drawing on Portuguese tile traditions, New Orleans porch culture and the Bauhaus geometry of his native Germany. The collection is characteristic of his method: every object begins as a story about a place, and the materials are chosen to carry that story home. In parallel, his ongoing work with the Ames Sala collection has become a landmark of ethical collaboration, translating the craft of Colombian weavers into contemporary furniture for a global audience.

His work has been widely honoured — including "Designer of the Year" by Maison&Objet in 2019, EDIDA and German Design Awards, and features in the permanent collections of the Vitra Design Museum, Die Neue Sammlung in Munich and the Pinakothek der Moderne. Yet what distinguishes Herkner is less the trophy cabinet than the tone of the work itself: unhurried, humane, quietly optimistic. In an industry often drawn to spectacle, he has made a compelling case for design as a slow conversation between maker, material and place — an approach that feels increasingly essential, and increasingly rare.$$
WHERE slug = 'sebastian-herkner-man-of-parts';